WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.801
Now that you have an idea about

2
00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.770
the goal of the knowledge graph you'll build,

3
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:07.140
let's go through the details of the multi-agent system you'll design

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.577
to first find the graph model and then construct the graph.

5
00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.980
There are lots of definitions for an agent. Here's my definition.

6
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.683
I like to think about agents from an engineering perspective.

7
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:19.686
And for me, it's a fancy new kind of control flow operator.

8
00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.022
Basically you have a loop, and inside of

9
00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.957
the loop there's a call to an LLM

10
00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.960
that then figures out what it wants to do,

11
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.661
and then based on what it wants to do,

12
00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:32.766
passes that back over to the computer side, to the client side,

13
00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:34.768
and really runs into a switch statement

14
00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.170
that performs potential actions.

15
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.773
So in this loop, this is really just

16
00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:43.176
intelligent behavior that's happening because of the LLM call,

17
00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:46.579
but the execution of that intelligent behavior is still happening through code.

18
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.148
What's great about this architecture, of

19
00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:49.682
course, is that agents are really powerful

20
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.384
because you've got an LLM there to

21
00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.386
do some reasoning and call out to tools

22
00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.655
that can do anything you can do with code.

23
00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.058
And it's adaptive because the LLM is given memory,

24
00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:01.861
so it can learn what's happened either through the conversation or through

25
00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.697
things that you've saved into memory that are important

26
00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.399
for it to decide future outcomes.

27
00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.401
And it's pretty easy to get started

28
00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.703
because you're really just doing natural language descriptions

29
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.906
through the prompts about what the agent should do.

30
00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:16.709
But all this power and ease comes with some downsides.

31
00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.211
It can be slow working with

32
00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.746
an agent because the calls out to

33
00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.115
a remote LLM is a bit expensive and slow.

34
00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:26.419
It's also non-deterministic, well, because LLMs are non-deterministic.

35
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.621
And, you know, token cost over time

36
00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.423
can really add up quite a lot.

37
00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.926
So if we're running an agent over time

38
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.728
in production for thousands and thousands of calls,

39
00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.431
the token cost gets really expensive really quickly.

40
00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.634
That's one of the advantages of moving to a multi-agent system

41
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.737
is that you can actually improve some of the downsides here.

42
00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.607
So what is exactly a multi-agent system?

43
00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.110
It is of course just a system where you have multiple agents

44
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.212
working collaboratively on a single goal.

45
00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:55.548
The agents typically are arranged into a kind of a hierarchy

46
00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.151
where you have a top-level agent that manages the overall flow,

47
00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:01.154
and then as many sub-level agents as you'd like to,

48
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:03.123
that have either different phases of the work

49
00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:05.525
or very specific tasks that they're performing.

50
00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.328
The agents interact in a couple different ways.

51
00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:12.599
There's a main conversational thread that's basically happening with the user

52
00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:14.100
where the user might have some message

53
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.602
that they kick off the work with.

54
00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:17.704
And then each of the agents can decide

55
00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.405
is this work that I can do

56
00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:21.107
or something that somebody else should do.

57
00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.409
So, can I delegate this to a different agent?

58
00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.212
So you can see in this illustration here that the root agent

59
00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.815
is able to delegate to either agent A or agent B.

60
00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:32.118
Internally, agent A and agent B can do some work

61
00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.786
using the tools that they've been given.

62
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.122
One of the tools in agent B is unique.

63
00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.291
This p agent here, this p function,

64
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.094
is actually going to be an agent itself,

65
00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:43.596
but being called as if it were a tool.

66
00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.465
So there's two ways for agents to

67
00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.300
interact, either agents delegating to each other

68
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.169
or an agent that's behaving as

69
00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.304
a tool being called by another agent.

70
00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.206
And along the way, you've got specific

71
00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.108
checkpoints with the user that are possible

72
00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.110
whenever you have any of these transitions.

73
00:02:57.644 --> 00:03:00.446
So what is the multi-agent system that you'll build?

74
00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.449
You'll build parts of a Knowledge Graph Agent,

75
00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:06.619
focusing on the sub-agents that perform knowledge graph construction.

76
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.423
You'll learn the basics of building an agent using Google's ADK.

77
00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.425
You'll learn what memory is and how

78
00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:14.427
to use it for recording critical information.

79
00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.629
You'll define some tools.

80
00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.631
And of course, all of this will

81
00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.633
converge from open-ended conversation to executable code.

82
00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.570
This beautiful diagram are all the agents

83
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.172
and the flow of agents and their interactions.

84
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.142
Let's walk through this a little bit at a time.

85
00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:31.578
At the top level, this is the agent

86
00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:33.646
that manages the overall introduction of the user

87
00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.949
to what's possible, what the agent's

88
00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:37.951
responsibility is,

89
00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:42.121
and helping the user understand what's the workflow that's possible here.

90
00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:45.725
from the knowledge graph construction all the way to doing graph retrieval.

91
00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:47.527
So this is really a conversational agent that

92
00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.128
itself is not going to do any work,

93
00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:51.397
but it's going to guide the user through the

94
00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.433
different phases of work that are going to occur.

95
00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.236
It's this next level of agents down

96
00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.538
that are really going to manage the individual workflows.

97
00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.740
And there are three major workflows.

98
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.443
If really on the left, we've got two workflows,

99
00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:06.145
the Structured Data Agent and the Unstructured Data Agent.

100
00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.549
These are responsible for taking the user through the idea

101
00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.217
of like what they might want to

102
00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.052
do, all the way to constructing a graph.

103
00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.054
And then, if those have done their job,

104
00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.123
this third agent, the GraphRAG Agent.

105
00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:19.959
on the right side is responsible for helping the user

106
00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.161
actually answer questions using that graph.

107
00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.497
Both the Structured Data Agent and

108
00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.666
the Unstructured Data Agent are workflow agents.

109
00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.135
They perform their work by interacting with the user

110
00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.903
and helping them go through the

111
00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.939
steps of what needs to happen from

112
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.340
the idea of what they want to

113
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.942
do all the way to describing the graph.

114
00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:38.344
And they do that by delegating to sub-agents

115
00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:40.346
that specialize in each phase of the workflow.

116
00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:42.649
You'll see some replication here

117
00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:44.951
that both the structured and unstructured data

118
00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.653
have some agents that are in common,

119
00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:47.954
and I'll touch on those as

120
00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.422
we get into the one level down.

121
00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.024
Okay, so here's the sub-agents

122
00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:53.626
that are actually doing the workflow for structured data.

123
00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:55.928
Let's walk through these one at a time

124
00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.031
and also what's important to note here

125
00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:00.933
is the output of actually these agents doing their work.

126
00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.536
If you think about yourself as being a data engineer

127
00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:06.239
that's been given the task of actually performing this work,

128
00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.342
each of these agents are kind of what you would do yourself

129
00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:10.943
if you're engaging in the work.

130
00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:14.647
This first one, the User Intent Agent, is what you would do

131
00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.450
when you've been asked to do some task for data analysis,

132
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.853
and whoever asked that, you respond to with some questions.

133
00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:23.356
Hey, could you clarify what you want me to do?

134
00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.159
Give me, you know, what are the goals you're after,

135
00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.127
what kind of analysis do you want me to perform?

136
00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.330
And it's really important to nail that down up front.

137
00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.132
This is the kind of the requirements or the direction overall

138
00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.435
of what the job is. So...

139
00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:37.937
this User Intent Agent, even though it's collaborative

140
00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:41.341
and kind of conversational, the output of this is really important.

141
00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:43.042
We're capturing what is the user goal

142
00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.244
and what are they trying to get out of this work.

143
00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:48.247
Based on that user goal then, this next agent down,

144
00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:51.351
the File Suggestion Agent, is going to take into account

145
00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:54.053
what has been established through the User Intent Agent

146
00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:57.256
as the direction and the goal of what the work is,

147
00:05:57.457 --> 00:05:58.424
and then it's going to look

148
00:05:58.424 --> 00:05:59.559
at the data files that are available.

149
00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.694
We'll try to find from those data files

150
00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:04.130
which ones are useful for actually achieving that goal.

151
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.499
There might be more files that are available, there

152
00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:08.568
might be even multiple data sources that are available.

153
00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.204
Here we're simplifying it down to just making

154
00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.373
suggestions for files that are available on disk.

155
00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.875
The output of that ends up being a list

156
00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:18.144
of suggested files that have been approved by the user.

157
00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:20.847
And then that gets input into the

158
00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:23.349
next agent, which is the Schema Proposal Agent.

159
00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.451
The Schema Proposal Agent is actually a

160
00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:27.453
pair of agents formed in a Critic Pattern

161
00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:30.456
where one of the agents is responsible for making a proposal

162
00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:31.858
about what the schema could look like,

163
00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:33.459
and the next one is going to be

164
00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.962
a critic saying, well, maybe that's not quite so right,

165
00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:38.464
think about changing it in this way or that way.

166
00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.466
We'll look at the details in a later lesson.

167
00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:43.970
But the idea here is that this pairing of agents

168
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.639
internally will loop through what's possible,

169
00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:48.374
self-criticize itself, and the outcome of

170
00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:50.343
that should be a good graph schema

171
00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:53.146
that uses the files that were approved from the previous agent

172
00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:55.014
and also in compliance with the user

173
00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:57.150
goals that were set by the first agent,

174
00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.252
producing a graph schema that can answer

175
00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.354
questions that are useful for the user.

176
00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:03.122
So the output of all of

177
00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.158
that, we're calling a Graph Construction Plan.

178
00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:07.160
It's not a graph itself,

179
00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.662
but it's a description about how to construct that graph.

180
00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:12.131
The second workflow in the middle,

181
00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:14.033
the first two parts of that workflow

182
00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.235
are the exact same as the structured data workflow.

183
00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:19.005
Here in the middle, we're dealing with unstructured data,

184
00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.507
but it still begins with understanding what is the user intent

185
00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:25.511
for bringing in the unstructured data, and here that's markdown files.

186
00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:27.980
And then okay, if you understand why the

187
00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.016
user wants to bring in some markdown files,

188
00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:32.518
you can identify which files are relevant.

189
00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.821
Again, the File Suggestion Agent would do that.

190
00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:37.223
Similar behavior to the structured data.

191
00:07:37.657 --> 00:07:39.759
But this third step is different.

192
00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.794
In the third step, rather than designing

193
00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.863
a schema based on some CSV files,

194
00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:45.865
all you have is text.

195
00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:48.434
And from that text, how do you create a graph?

196
00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:49.836
Well, the approach here is that

197
00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:51.437
we're going to have two specialized agents

198
00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:54.841
that look through the text and identify what are called entities,

199
00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:58.444
so people, places, and things that occur within the text,

200
00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:00.847
and also for those entities, it identifies

201
00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:04.650
what facts are describing those entities within the text.

202
00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:08.054
So, let's say that it was a, you know, I was

203
00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:11.090
in the text and I had some reviews of local coffee shops.

204
00:08:11.591 --> 00:08:13.526
It might reveal that, you know, ABK

205
00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.495
really loves to score coffee from Phil's.

206
00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:18.130
Because who doesn't? So those are facts

207
00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.066
that could be extracted from the text.

208
00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:22.235
The goal of this agent is actually to

209
00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:24.070
find out what kinds of facts can be extracted

210
00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.241
and not do the extraction but simply describe what's possible.

211
00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:31.644
And so that we're going to be calling a Knowledge Extraction Plan.

212
00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:35.047
The Knowledge Extraction Plan paired with the Graph Construction Plan

213
00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:38.251
from the structured data, together give all the rules that we need

214
00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:41.254
using the Graph Construction Plan and the Knowledge Extraction Plan.

215
00:08:41.654 --> 00:08:43.623
This red box down in the corner then is

216
00:08:43.623 --> 00:08:45.625
just a single tool that is multiple tools internally.

217
00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:47.727
that actually can take those plans

218
00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:51.297
and do the extraction itself and do the construction itself.

219
00:08:51.631 --> 00:08:55.201
It'll loop over all the construction rules to create a domain graph.

220
00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:57.803
loop over all the Markdown files, chunk them up,

221
00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.906
do the vector embedding of those,

222
00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:02.808
and then also extract entities and the facts,

223
00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:05.411
and then connect those to the structured data.

224
00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.680
In lessons 4 through 8, you'll go

225
00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.615
through the entire workflow for structured data,

226
00:09:10.316 --> 00:09:14.186
from user intent, file suggestions, to the schema proposal.

227
00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:17.557
Then, we'll skip over to the unstructured data,

228
00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:19.692
just the entity and fact type proposal.

229
00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:23.262
And then finally, we'll look at the particular tool in lesson 8

230
00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:24.964
that does the graph construction itself.

231
00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.466
It does all the hard work that the previous agents really

232
00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:29.835
did the reasoning about what the work should be.

233
00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:32.071
In the next lesson, we'll actually get

234
00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:34.040
into doing some coding, building your first agent.

235
00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:36.342
We're going to be using Google's ADK.

236
00:09:36.542 --> 00:09:38.177
If you're already familiar with Google

237
00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:40.046
ADK, you could skip over that lesson

238
00:09:40.046 --> 00:09:42.348
and move on to actually building the multi-agent system.

239
00:09:42.548 --> 00:09:44.550
But I think it's a really good overview

240
00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.319
and also it helps you understand how I

241
00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:48.054
think about it and how I've implemented it

242
00:09:48.054 --> 00:09:50.356
in this particular multi-agent system.

243
00:09:50.556 --> 00:09:53.159
So it's nice to walk through. I'll see you there.
